// Physics, in quantities that carry their units.
//
//   use "/lib/phys"
//
// Every function here takes and answers a `qty`, so a unit error is an
// **error** rather than a plausible number. `ke(qty(2, "kg"), qty(3, "s"))` is
// refused by name, because `kg * s^2` is not an energy -- and that refusal
// happens in the arithmetic rather than in a check somebody remembered to
// write. That is the whole reason this library exists, and it is the same
// class of bug the ACPI battery code hit for real: milliamp-hours divided by
// watts gives a number that looks like a time and is not one.
//
// A formula here is therefore checkable twice over: against a textbook, and
// against its own dimensions. `unit(ke(...))` is `m^2*kg/s^2`, which is a
// joule whether or not the arithmetic is right.
//
// ### The range, which is a real limit and is stated rather than found
//
// A magnitude is an exact fraction of two `i64`s, so it spans about 10^-18 to
// 10^18 and no further. Human-scale physics fits with room. Atomic-scale
// physics does not, and the constants that cannot be held are named below
// rather than approximated into something that looks exact:
//
//   fits      c, g, G, R, atmosphere, astronomical unit, light year
//   does not  h (6.6e-34), k_B (1.4e-23), N_A (6.0e23), e (1.6e-19),
//             the electron and proton masses, the permittivity of free space
//
// `h` needs a denominator of 10^42 and there is no honest way to write that
// here: the nearest whole fraction that fits is not Planck's constant, it is
// a different number that would then be used as though it were. `si` below
// builds one of these if you want to try, and **fails loudly** with
// "numerator overflowed" rather than answering.
//
// There is a consolation and it is a real one. `R` is the gas constant and
// `R = N_A * k_B` exactly, so everything molar -- the ideal gas law, molar
// heat capacity, the whole of the chemistry a person does with a balance and
// a thermometer -- is reachable at full precision. What is out of range is
// the per-particle view, which is the view that needs the constants that do
// not fit.
//
// ### Everything is a function, including the constants
//
// Deliberately: a top level of nothing but declarations is what
// `Interp::prepare` requires to run a program's top level once and copy the
// result. A single top-level assignment here would make every program that
// imports this file non-preparable, which is a cost paid somewhere else
// entirely for a spelling that saves two characters.

// --- constants ----------------------------------------------------------

// The speed of light, **exact by definition** since 1983: the metre is
// defined from it, so this is not a measurement that could be refined.
fn c() { return qty(299792458, "m/s") }

// Standard gravity, also exact by definition. Not the local value anywhere;
// the agreed conventional one, used to define the kilogram-force.
fn g() { return qty(rat(196133, 20000), "m/s^2") }

// The molar gas constant, exact since the 2019 redefinition made both N_A and
// k_B exact. 8.31446261815324 J/(mol*K), written as the fraction it is.
fn R() { return qty(rat(831446261815324, 100000000000000), "m^2*kg/s^2*mol*K") }

// Newton's constant. **The one measured number here**, and the least
// precisely known constant in physics -- about five significant figures,
// where everything else above is exact. 6.67430e-11.
fn G() { return qty(rat(667430, 10000000000000000), "m^3/kg*s^2") }

// One standard atmosphere, exact by definition.
fn atm() { return qty(101325, "Pa") }

// The ice point, for converting a Celsius temperature to an absolute one.
fn T0() { return qty(rat(27315, 100), "K") }

fn celsius(t) { return qty(t, "K") + T0() }

// The astronomical unit and the light year, both exact by definition -- the
// second being the first multiplied out over a Julian year.
fn au() { return qty(149597870700, "m") }
fn ly() { return qty(9460730472580800, "m") }

fn minute() { return qty(60, "s") }
fn hour() { return qty(3600, "s") }
fn day() { return qty(86400, "s") }
fn year() { return qty(31557600, "s") }

// A quantity written the way a constant is printed: m * 10^e of some unit.
//
// The failure is the feature. `si(662607015, -42, "m^2*kg/s")` is Planck's
// constant and this machine cannot hold it, so the call **errors** where a
// library that quietly rounded would hand back something usable and wrong.
fn si(m: int, e: int, u) {
  return qty(m * pow(10, e), u)
}

// --- mechanics ----------------------------------------------------------

fn speed(d, t) { return d / t }
fn accel(dv, t) { return dv / t }
fn force(m, a) { return m * a }
fn mom(m, v) { return m * v }
fn work(f, d) { return f * d }
fn pwr(w, t) { return w / t }

// Kinetic energy, one half m v squared. The half is `rat(1, 2)` and not
// `/ 2`: a mass of 1 kg divided by 2 in whole-number arithmetic is 0 kg.
fn ke(m, v) { return rat(1, 2) * m * v * v }

fn pe(m, h) { return m * g() * h }

fn dens(m, v) { return m / v }
fn pres(f, a) { return f / a }

// Newton's law of gravitation. Worth one claim on its own, because the
// dimension of `G * kg * kg / m^2` coming out as a newton is not obvious by
// inspection and is exactly what a unit system is for.
fn grav(m1, m2, r) { return G() * m1 * m2 / (r * r) }

// --- electricity --------------------------------------------------------

fn ohm_v(i, r) { return i * r }
fn ohm_i(v, r) { return v / r }
fn ohm_r(v, i) { return v / i }
fn pelec(v, i) { return v * i }

// --- gases --------------------------------------------------------------

// The ideal gas law, solved for each variable in turn. Four functions rather
// than one with a flag, because each is a formula somebody looks up and the
// dimensions of each are a separate claim.
fn gas_p(n, t, v) { return n * R() * t / v }
fn gas_v(n, t, p) { return n * R() * t / p }
fn gas_n(p, v, t) { return p * v / (R() * t) }
fn gas_t(p, v, n) { return p * v / (n * R()) }

// Moles from a mass and a molar mass. No table of elements here: one would be
// a hundred lines of data that `tools/` should generate rather than a person
// type, and nothing has asked for it yet.
fn moles(mass, molar) { return mass / molar }

// --- the square root of a quantity --------------------------------------

// The root of a quantity, with the expected unit supplied by the caller.
//
// **The unit is asserted rather than derived, and it has to be.** A dimension
// is seven whole exponents, so the root of `m/s` would need exponents of a
// half and there is nowhere to put them; and even where the exponents are
// even, an approximate magnitude cannot carry a unit at all -- `real` refuses
// a quantity by name.
//
// So this works exactly when the answer is exactly representable, and says so
// by **checking**: the candidate is squared and compared against the input,
// which settles both halves at once. A wrong unit fails. A magnitude that is
// not a perfect square fails. Nothing approximate is ever returned wearing a
// unit it did not earn.
//
//     qsqrt(qty(9, "m^2/s^2"), "m/s")   ->  3 m/s
//     qsqrt(qty(2, "m^2"), "m")         ->  nothing
fn qsqrt(x, u) {
  m = mag(x)
  r = qty(rat(sqrt(num(m)), sqrt(den(m))), u)
  if (r * r == x) { return r }
  return
}

// Escape velocity, which is the reason `qsqrt` exists: sqrt(2GM/r) has
// dimension m/s, and both the dimension and the magnitude have to come out
// whole for an exact answer. Nothing when they do not, which is most of the
// time -- so this is honest about being a special case rather than a general
// formula.
fn escape(m, r, u) { return qsqrt(2 * G() * m / r, u) }
